    <div class="mt-16 max-w-6xl mx-auto w-full">
        <h3 class="text-xl font-bold mb-6 flex items-center gap-2 text-brand-text">
            {% icon "fa-solid-network-wired", "text-brand-accent" %} The app behind this site
        </h3>

        <div class="bg-brand-surface border border-brand-border p-6 sm:p-8 rounded-xl">
            <p class="text-brand-muted leading-relaxed max-w-3xl">
                app.tzortzoglou.eu signs people in, pulls from 24 outside services and stores data in Postgres. I
                built it and run it. It's the same job as a customer onboarding: getting systems that weren't built
                for each other to agree on what a field means.
            </p>

            <div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
                {% include "chrome/architecture.njk" %}
            </div>
            {# Filled by scripts/arch-live.js from the status page's data; a reserved height, so the
               words arriving do not move what is below. #}
            <p id="archLive" class="mt-4 min-h-[3.75rem] sm:min-h-[1.25rem] text-xs text-brand-muted" aria-live="polite"></p>

            <div class="mt-10 grid gap-6 md:grid-cols-2">
                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-text">24 sources, one format</h4>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-base text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
                        Weather, Swedish timetables, bus positions, Formula 1, GitHub, sports leagues and newspapers
                        all describe things differently. I map 36 fields into one format and mark calculated values,
                        like a delay in minutes, so nobody mistakes them for measurements.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-text">A two-hour bug that wasn't the vendor's</h4>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-base text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
                        Every bus showed two hours late. The timetable feed sends Swedish local time with no time zone,
                        and the server runs in UTC. The API now reads those times as Stockholm time, with tests for
                        both daylight-saving switches.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-text">Access is checked on every request</h4>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-base text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
                        A login token proves who you are, not what you're allowed to do right now. So each request
                        checks your role in the database first. If I remove someone's access, it stops on their next
                        click, not when their token expires.
                    </p>
                </div>

                <div>
                    <h4 class="font-bold text-brand-text">A database viewer with no query box</h4>
                    <p class="mt-2 text-base text-brand-muted leading-relaxed">
                        Admins can read an approved list of tables. There's nowhere to type SQL, and IP address
                        columns are never loaded. A stolen admin session can read tables but can't run queries.
                    </p>
                </div>
            </div>

            <p class="mt-8 text-base text-brand-muted">
