<figure class="contrib bg-brand-surface border border-brand-border p-6 rounded-xl shadow-lg w-full overflow-x-auto">
    <figcaption class="sr-only">
        {{ contributions.total }} GitHub contributions between {{ contributions.from }} and
        {{ contributions.to }}, shown as a grid of days shaded by how many contributions each
        holds, stronger squares meaning more.
    </figcaption>
    <div class="contrib-layout text-brand-muted">
        <div></div>
        <div class="contrib-months" aria-hidden="true">
            {%- for month in contributions.months %}
            <span>{{ month }}</span>
            {%- endfor %}
        </div>
        <div class="contrib-days" aria-hidden="true">
            <span></span><span>Mon</span><span></span><span>Wed</span><span></span><span>Fri</span><span></span>
        </div>
        <div class="contrib-grid">
            {%- for week in contributions.weeks %}{% for day in week %}
            {%- if day %}<div class="contrib-day" data-level="{{ day.level }}" title="{{ day.label }}"></div>
            {%- else %}<div></div>
            {%- endif %}
            {%- endfor %}{% endfor %}
        </div>
    </div>
    {# A key, because a colour ramp with nothing naming its ends is five shades of nothing. The
       convention is borrowed from GitHub and widely read, which is an argument for keeping the
       ramp and not for leaving it unlabelled - a reader who has never seen one of these grids
       has no way in.

       The whole paragraph is aria-hidden, not just the swatches, because "Fewer" and "More" are
       positional labels: read out without the five boxes between them they are the string
       "Fewer More", which is worse than silence. What they mean is already carried twice in
       language - by the sentence below ("a stronger square being a busier day") and by the
       figure's own sr-only caption ("stronger squares meaning more") - so nothing is lost by hiding the row.
       The caption used to say only that the grid was "shaded by how many contributions each
       holds", which is the encoding without its direction; the three words were added so that
       this paragraph's claim of twice is true, and so that editing the visible sentence cannot
       take the direction away from a screen reader entirely. It is the direction
       of the ramp that is repeated, not these two words.

       The same key is under the hour grid on /admin/analytics/, drawn from this same ramp. #}
    <p class="mt-4 flex items-center gap-2 text-xs text-brand-muted" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>Fewer</span>
        <span class="flex gap-[3px]">
            <span class="contrib-day h-3 w-3"></span>
            <span class="contrib-day h-3 w-3" data-level="1"></span>
            <span class="contrib-day h-3 w-3" data-level="2"></span>
            <span class="contrib-day h-3 w-3" data-level="3"></span>
            <span class="contrib-day h-3 w-3" data-level="4"></span>
        </span>
        <span>More</span>
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 text-base text-brand-muted">
        {{ contributions.total }} contributions in the last year, a stronger square being a busier
        day.
        <a href="{{ contributions.profile }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer"
            class="text-brand-accent underline hover:no-underline">See the profile on GitHub</a>.
    </p>
</figure>
{% else %}
